==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|lla|nudius}} {{m|la|tertius}}, from the phrase ''[[nunc]] [[dies]] [[tertius]] [[est]]'', (now is the third day). Coined by {{w|Nathaniel Ward}} in 1647, ''The Simple Cobler of Aggawam in America''.<ref>'''2004''', David Mellinkoff, ''The Language of the Law'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=YBRLAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA207&dq=%22nudiustertian%22&hl=en&sa=X&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22nudiustertian%22&f=false page 207] − Ward coined ''nugiperous'' from Latin ''nugae'' (nonsense or foolish), and also ''nudiustertian'' from Latin ''nudius tertius'' (day before yesterday). Both promptly became obsolete.</ref>

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{context|rare|obsolete|lang=en}} Of the [[day before yesterday]].
#* '''1647''', {{w|Nathaniel Ward}}, ''The Simple Cobler of Aggawam in America'', 2011, [http://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/34974 Gutenberg EBook #34974],
#*: {{...}}but when I heare a nugiperous Gentledame inquire what dresse the Queen is in this week: what the '''nudiustertian''' fashion of the Court; I meane the very newest:{{...}}.

